# Lab notes

## What worked

- **Auxiliary variables as the data model.** The derivatives could be modelled
  as exahype2.api.AuxiliaryVariables without any change to ExaHyPE. The rkfd4 scheme
  already hands the solver's auxiliary variables to the kernels in the same
  array as the unknowns (unknowns first, then the auxiliary variables), which
  is exactly the layout of the 59 CCZ4 entries. Evolving only 25 of them needs
  nothing besides the existing "copy only the unknowns back" logic.
- **One shared time stepping graph.** The Project owns one time stepping graph
  into which all solvers insert their tasks. The reconstructor looks up the
  solver's tasks by name ("<solver>_Reconstruct stage <s>") and adds itself as
  an additional dependency. No change of the generic exahype2.api was required.
- **Reusing ReconstructPatchAndApplyFunctor.** The toolbox action set builds the
  halo patch and then runs a user-supplied snippet. The snippet is one call
  into the C++ library, i.e. the generated code stays tiny.
- **Refactoring FOCCZ4Solver.** After moving the logic into the base class,
  the unit tests of the first-order solver (tests/unit/test_exagrype_ccz4_solver.py,
  test_exagrype_fd4_with_ko_dissipation.py) passed unchanged.

## What did not work (initially), and how we fixed it

- **The reconstructor as a second exahype2.api.Solver / project member.**
  The original idea was to add the reconstructor to the project like a solver.
  The project's repository generator expects every solver to have a C++ solver
  class and a global instance (get_name_of_solver_class() etc.). The
  reconstructor evolves nothing and has no such class. Giving it one would have
  meant a dummy numerical scheme, dummy time stepping and an unnecessary C++
  class. Instead, the SO solver creates its reconstructor in the constructor
  and realises it right after itself. Users only add the SO solver to the
  project. Conceptually, the pair is still two partners (the reconstructor has
  its own tasks, data and name prefix).
- **Peano's stacks and the face data.** The first implementation declared the
  faces of the derivatives as WRITE in the reconstructor's task. In a debug
  build, Peano aborted in the first time step in
  SpacetreeSet::exchangeAllPeriodicBoundaryDataStacks() with
  "stackContainer.getForPush(...)->empty()" (a stack of 81 entries was not
  empty). Rule of thumb: every face (or cell) datum that has been written
  once has to be loaded (read) by the next task that touches it, as otherwise
  the entries pile up on Peano's stacks. The setup writes the derivatives'
  faces, therefore the reconstructor has to declare them READ_AND_WRITE
  (it loads the old value, the roll-over overwrites it). The stage input
  needs READ_AND_WRITE for the same reason, as the solver's reconstruction
  reads it afterwards.
- **Two rkfd4 solvers in one project.** Both solvers write the solver-independent
  kernel kernels/rungekutta_linear_combination.cpph, and the generator appends to
  the file. The second solver therefore duplicates the function
  (redefinition error). The same happens if you re-run a generator in an
  already populated directory (also the solver-specific kernels are duplicated
  then). The benchmark works around it by building FO and SO in separate
  directories, and deleting the directory first. A proper fix, i.e.
  making exahype2.api.rungekutta.generate_linear_combination_kernel()
  idempotent, is outside of this project's scope and left to a decision of the
  maintainers.
- **enforceCCZ4constraints() on 25 unknowns.** The routine touches entries up to
  index 54 and would read/write beyond the 25 unknowns of the SO patch. We
  split it (see above). The first-order solver's behaviour is bit-identical.
- **Logging.** With LoggerVerbosity.TEXT, debug builds log every data movement
  (several hundred thousand lines per second). A benchmark that runs for a
  few time steps then takes forever. For tests use LoggerVerbosity.OFF and the
  release build.

## Known limitations

- Only FD4 (exahype2.api.rkfd4.CentralDifferencesWithRungeKutta and
  exagrype.api.FD4WithKODissipation) works yet. The FV variants are open.
- The derivatives are recomputed in front of every Runge-Kutta stage, from the
  stage's input. The maximum eigenvalue of the new solution (needed for the next
  time step size) is computed from the derivatives of the last stage, i.e.
  not from the final solution.
- The reconstruction of the derivatives in the first layer of the halo is
  the one of the neighbour cell (exchanged via the faces). It is therefore
  consistent across cell boundaries, unlike the legacy approach, which computed
  the derivatives of the halo from the (incomplete) halo of the primary
  variables.
- Boundary conditions other than periodic ones, adaptive meshes, checkpoints
  and tracers have not been tested with the SO solver.


# Benchmarks

See the benchmark benchmarks/exagrype/fo_vs_so. It runs the gauge wave
with both solvers and prints the maximum and relative L2 difference of the 25
evolved unknowns at the end of the run.

Setup (`python3 fo_vs_so.py`, but with a release build and end time 0.1 instead of the debug defaults): gauge wave on the
periodic unit cube, 27 grid points per axis (patch size 9, 3 patches per axis), fourth-order
Finite Differences with classic Runge-Kutta 4 and lopsided advection, no dissipation,
adaptive time step size (CFL), end time 0.1 (about 100 time steps). Both solvers
use the same numerical scheme. We compare the 25 evolved unknowns at the end of the
run. The last column shows how far the first-order solution moved away from its
initial condition, i.e. the scale of the dynamics.

| unknown | max abs. difference SO vs. FO | relative L2 difference | max abs. change of FO in the run |
|---|---|---|---|
| G (xx, yy, zz) | 7.6e-6 / 4.1e-6 / 4.1e-6 | 3.4e-6 / 1.8e-6 / 1.8e-6 | 4.2e-2 / 2.1e-2 / 2.1e-2 |
| K (xx, yy, zz) | 7.7e-5 / 4.1e-5 / 4.1e-5 | 2.3e-4 | 1.4e-1 / 7.9e-2 / 7.9e-2 |
| lapse | 2.5e-6 | 1.0e-6 | 3.1e-2 |
| phi | 2.0e-6 | 8.7e-7 | 1.1e-2 |
| traceK | 1.3e-4 | 2.4e-4 | 2.3e-1 |
| Z (x) | 5.0e-5 | 7.7e-5 | 3.1e-1 |
| theta | 3.6e-5 | (not meaningful) | 1.9e-5 |
| all others (off-diagonals, shift, b, Z_y, Z_z) | 0 | 0 | 0 |

**Reading the table.** The two solvers agree to 1e-6 to 1e-4 in absolute terms,
which is two to four orders of magnitude below the change of the solution within
the run. The metric, the lapse and phi agree best (relative 1e-6), the extrinsic
curvature and its trace worst (relative 2e-4). The remaining entries are
identically zero for both (the gauge wave does not excite them). Theta (a measure of the
Hamilton constraint violation) is tiny in the first-order run (it changes by 1.9e-5
at most), and the two solvers differ by 3.6e-5, i.e. by more than the first-order
solver's theta moves. The relative difference is therefore not meaningful. We have
no reference solution for theta yet, so we cannot say which solver is closer to the
truth.

**Caveats.** One resolution, one end time, one test case. The comparison measures
how close SO is to FO, not how accurate either of them is. A convergence study
against the analytical gauge wave (@ref benchmarks_exagrype_gauge_wave), a
longer run, and a black hole run are still missing.

**Cost.** In the release build with 8 OpenMP threads (machine shared with other
jobs, so treat with care), one reported TimeStep phase took about 0.15 s for the
first-order solver and about 0.21 s for the second-order solver on this mesh,
i.e. SO is about 40% slower per step. This is expected: SO reconstructs the
derivatives in an extra mesh sweep per Runge-Kutta stage, exchanges an extra
halo of 34 values per stage, and gathered/scattered the 59 entries in every PDE term
evaluation (it does not anymore, the kernels take the primary and the secondary
entries as two pointers). We did not profile it. SO stores fewer values per stage (25 instead of
59 for the stage input, the right-hand-side estimates and the halo patch). A
first, finer run (729 patches of 9^3 points) was killed by the operating system
for the first-order solver (out of memory, 15 GB machine shared with other
processes), while the second-order solver ran through with a resident set of
about 3.5 GB. This is an observation, not a measurement.

*/
